 Play T9, Play T3, Play B1, Play B2, Play J3, Play T6, Play B3, Play B2, Play T9.\nThe game history of the previous player is: Play B7, Play F3, Play B8, Peng F2, Play J1, Play J3, Play J2, Play W8, Play W4, Play F1, Play F4, Play T2, Play T9, Play W9, Play W3, Play B4, Play W1, Play W5, Play W8, Play B3.\nThe followings are your valid actions:[Play T7; Play W6; Play T2]\u3002", "reasoning": "", "output": "Evaluation: My current hand: [W6, T2, T2, T7, T7] plus three melds: Chow [W6, W7, W8], Chow [W3, W4, W5], and Pung [T6, T6, T6]. The hand is incomplete; I need a pair and one more meld to win. The three tile types in hand are W6 (1 copy), T2 (2 copies), and T7 (2 copies). Possible promising moves:  \n- Play T7: keeps the T2 pair and the single W6, aiming to complete a meld with W6 (could pair with another W6, or form a Chow with W4,W5 or W7,W8, but W4/W5/W7/W8 are scarce).  \n- Play W6: keeps the T2 pair and the T7 pair, giving two pairs which is not ideal for standard hand (needs one pair only), but could aim for Seven Pairs? But I have three exposed melds already, so Seven Pairs is impossible because exposed melds disqualify it. Must aim for standard 4 melds + pair. So having two pairs means I need to convert one into a meld.  \n- Play T2: keeps W6 and T7 pair; then I need another tile to make a meld with W6 and find a pair.  \n\nConsidering tile availability:  \nUnshown tiles: W6:1 left, T2:2 left, T7:4 left, also other tiles. Playing T7 discards a tile with 4 left, which is relatively safe. Playing W6 discards the only remaining W6, which might be riskier if someone needs it. Playing T2 discards a tile with 2 left.  \n\nMy hand potential: The melds are all suited tiles, no honors. I have two Chows in W suit and one Pung in T suit. The remaining tiles are mixed. To complete a hand, I need a pair and a meld. If I keep T2 pair, I need a meld from W6 (maybe pair W6 and draw another W6, or form a Chow with W4/W5 or W7/W8). If I keep T7 pair, I need a meld from W6 similarly. If I keep both T2 and T7 pairs, I have two pairs, so I need to dissolve one into a meld, meaning I need a third T2 or T7 to make a Pung, or a Chow involving T2/T7. T2 can form Chow T1T2T3 or T2T3T4, but T1 is gone (0 left), T3 has 3 left, T4 has 2 left, so possible. T7 can form Chow T5T6T7 or T6T7T8 or T7T8T9, but T6 is mostly used (0 left, but I have a pung of T6, so unavailable), T8 has 4 left, T9 is 0, T5 has 4 left. So T7 has more Chow possibilities (T5T6T7 needs T5, T6 is not available from wall because I have a pung, but could use my own T6? But T6 is in a pung, not in hand. So T7 Chow options: T5T6T7 (T5 available, T6 not available), T6T7T8 (T6 not available, T8 available), T7T8T9 (T9 not available). Actually, for Chow, tiles must come from hand or discard, but since T6 is already in a pung, I cannot use it to form a Chow with T7 unless I break the pung, which is not allowed. So forming a Chow with T7 is limited. Better to keep T2 pair and try to get a meld with W6 or draw a useful tile.  \n\nGiven the game state, I should aim for a quick win by discarding the least useful tile. W6 is isolated; T7 has pair potential but also could become part of a meld if I draw T5 or T8. However, with T6 pung, T7 cannot form a Chow using T6 because T6 is already used. But I could form a Chow T7T8T9 if I get T8 and T9, but T9 is 0 left. So T7 is not promising for Chow. T2 similarly: Chow T1T2T3 (T1 gone), T2T3T4 possible with T3 and T4 (some left). So T2 has some Chow potential.  \n\nThus, discarding T7 seems best because it preserves the T2 pair and keeps W6 as a flexible tile. Also, T7 has many left (4), so it's safer to discard.  \n\nReason: I have a T2 pair which is a good pair candidate. Discarding T7 reduces the hand to two tile types (W6 and T2 pair), making it easier to complete a meld with W6 (either pair it or form a Chow). T7 has low meld potential due to limited Chow options given the T6 pung and missing T9. Tile safety: T7 has 4 unseen, so less likely to give someone a pung/kong.  \n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
